# Vulnerability details

## Impact

```
listOfOptions.push(StrikeAndMaturity({strike: strike, maturity: maturity}));
```
in the function `addOptionEnumerationIfNecessary`, which is called in multiple other functions. This line of code pushes a new option to the `listOfOptions` array, but there is no mechanism to prevent the array from growing indefinitely which could cause out-of-gas issues or other problems.

Can lead to several issues, the most notable of which is the potential for an `out-of-gas exception`. An `out-of-gas exception` occurs when a transaction runs out of gas before it is able completed. In this case, if the `listOfOptions` array grows too large, it can consume all of the available gas during a transaction, causing it to fail with an out-of-gas exception. 


## Proof of Concept

An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by repeatedly calling the `addOptionEnumerationIfNecessary` function, causing the `listOfOptions` array to grow indefinitely.

Code.

```
pragma solidity =0.8.8;

contract Attacker {
    function attack(address target) public {
        for (uint i = 0; i < 10**20; i++) {
            TimeswapV2Option(target).addOptionEnumerationIfNecessary(i, i);
        }
    }
}
```
This attacker contract calls the `addOptionEnumerationIfNecessary` function in a loop, passing a different strike and maturity value each time. Because there is no mechanism to prevent the array from growing indefinitely, this will cause the `listOfOptions` array to grow indefinitely, consuming all the available gas and causing the transaction to fail with an `out-of-gas` exception.

